ghpull:720 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "Join reordering: benchmark whether cyclic/star BGPs are still a bottleneck (#507)" ;
    bl:number 720 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:507 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr720 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:507 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-10-22T17:01:30Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-22T17:29:21Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr720Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #507 asked whether genuinely cyclic/star BGPs (the shape Phase D / #39 would fix) are still a real bottleneck after Phases A-C landed. An AST-based scan of all 105 DBLP benchmark queries, using proper hypergraph (GYO) acyclicity rather than a pairwise-clique model, found zero cyclic join graphs in the suite - every \"star\" query is a tree on one shared variable, not a cycle, and the clique model had produced 8 false positives (e.g. flagging the single-pattern '?s ?p ?o .' COUNT query). A synthetic adversarial triangle (hub vertex + independent closing edges) confirmed the O(n^2) intermediate-row ceiling is real when exercised (measured, not asserted: rows_out matched n^2+n+2k exactly across n=200/500/1000). A realistic LUBM-Q9-shaped triangle showed non-adversarial cyclic queries avoid the blowup under Phase A's existing ordering. benches/lubm.rs's own generator could not be reused for the Q9 test as-is - its takesCourse/teacherOf objects are disjoint by construction, so the literal query would always return 0 rows against it - a deliberately overlapping dataset was built instead. Recommendation posted to #39: keep Phase D parked, since the benchmark corpus this repo actually measures against contains no instance of the shape it would help with." ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr720Decision ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:720 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr720 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr720Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Modeled each BGP's join graph as a hypergraph (one hyperedge per triple pattern, connecting all its variables at once) and tested acyclicity via the standard GYO reduction (drop subset hyperedges, drop ears, repeat to fixpoint)." ;
    agp:alternative "First draft modeled each pattern's variables as a pairwise clique of ordinary edges instead. This produced 8 false positives on the DBLP suite (any single pattern with 3 distinct variables, like '?s ?p ?o .', looks like a triangle under the clique model even though it is one atom, not a join between atoms). Rejected once the false positives were found; GYO correctly treats a single hyperedge as inherently acyclic." .
